.TH SYNCSPACE 1 "@RELEASE_DATE@" "SyncSpace @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
syncspace \- manage the SyncSpace local file-transfer service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syncspace
.RI [ open | start | stop | restart | status | logs | enable | disable ]
.br
.B syncspace
.RI [ --help | --version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
SyncSpace sends files directly between trusted devices on the same local
network. The packaged application runs as a systemd user service, keeping its
identity, trust records, settings, and received files inside the current user
account.
.PP
Running
.B syncspace
without an argument starts the service and opens its loopback-only web
interface. The encrypted peer listener is separate from the management
interface.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B open
Start SyncSpace, wait for its local interface, and open it in the default
browser. This is the default command.
.TP
.B start
Start SyncSpace for the current login session without opening a browser.
.TP
.B stop
Stop SyncSpace for the current user.
.TP
.B restart
Restart SyncSpace and apply changes from the optional environment file.
.TP
.B status
Show status and recent service output.
.TP
.B logs
Follow logs from the systemd user journal.
.TP
.B enable
Start SyncSpace and opt in to automatic startup at future logins.
.TP
.B disable
Stop SyncSpace and disable automatic startup.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Optional service overrides are read from
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/syncspace/service.env ,
or
.I $HOME/.config/syncspace/service.env
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset. An example is installed at
.IR /usr/share/doc/syncspace/service.env.example .
.PP
The management host must remain a loopback address. SyncSpace rejects a
non-loopback management binding even when it is supplied in the environment
file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/SyncSpace
Default identity, trust database, settings, and transfer working data.
.TP
.I $HOME/Downloads/SyncSpace
Default received-file directory for a new profile.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/syncspace.service
Packaged systemd user unit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
